decaf448 — third-party attribution
====================================================================

This file carries a CONDITION, added 2026-09-09. `src/kat_vectors.zig`
reproduces the test vectors published in RFC 9496 Appendix B. Redistributing
this module — alone, or as part of zig-libs — must keep this file with it.

WHY THIS FILE DID NOT EXIST UNTIL NOW. `README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement
answers a different question — it is about this module's CODE ("pure clean-room
from RFC 9496", with `std.crypto.ecc.Ristretto255` consulted for API shape) —
and says nothing about the vectors the module also ships. The gate written for
exactly this shape could not see them: `check-catalog`'s data-file claim looked
for a committed file that is neither Zig source nor documentation, and these
vectors were extracted from the RFC text into a `.zig` file, so no witness was
found and the check never ran. That hole was closed on 2026-09-09; this file is
one of the four records it forced.

No code changed, and none needs to. The vectors are unaltered.

WHAT WAS TAKEN
--------------

  - `src/kat_vectors.zig` — the decaf448 test vectors of RFC 9496 Appendix B,
    extracted programmatically from the RFC text at scaffold time and diffed
    byte-for-byte against it, not hand-transcribed. Whitespace and line
    wrapping were removed; no value was otherwise changed.

THE GRANT THIS RESTS ON
-----------------------

Not the merger doctrine. RFC test vectors are Code Components in the sense of
the IETF Trust Legal Provisions (BCP 78) — the Trust's list of Code Components
names "tables of values" explicitly — and TLP 5.0 §4.c grants them under the
Revised BSD License. A written grant is available at no cost, so this module
takes it rather than arguing that a table of values is uncopyrightable. That is
the same choice `modules/mls/NOTICE` made on 2026-09-08 and the one
`modules/ctap2pin/NOTICE` already cited.

